.\" Manual page for gputop-go.
.\"
.\" Every option documented here is registered in cmd/gputop/main.go, and the
.\" version matches the binary's default. scripts/check_packaging.sh fails when
.\" a flag is missing from this page or from one of the shell completions.
.TH GPUTOP 1 "2026-10-19" "gputop 1.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
gputop \- btop-style terminal monitor for AMD Radeon GPUs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gputop
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B gputop
\fB\-\-dump\fR [\fB\-\-pretty\fR] [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B gputop
\fB\-\-devices\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B gputop
\fB\-\-check\fR [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Reads what the amdgpu kernel driver already publishes (utilisation, clocks,
temperatures, power, throttle reasons, per-process engine time and memory)
and shows it in one screen. With no options it starts the terminal interface;
the headless modes make the same data scriptable.
.PP
Monitoring is read-only by construction. gputop never opens a sysfs or procfs
path for writing and never changes clocks, power limits or profiles, so it is
safe to leave running beside a game, a compute job or a display server.
.PP
Absent readings are shown as \fBN/A\fR (or an empty cell in a recording), never
as zero. A partial process view is labelled as partial: without root, other
users' \fIfdinfo\fR files are unreadable and the table says so.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-config\fR \fIPATH\fR
Read configuration from PATH instead of the default search locations.
.TP
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-interval\fR \fISECONDS\fR
Sampling interval, overriding \fIinterval_ms\fR from the configuration.
.TP
\fB\-\-drm-root\fR \fIPATH\fR
Read the DRM class directory at PATH instead of /sys/class/drm.
.TP
\fB\-\-proc-root\fR \fIPATH\fR
Read procfs at PATH instead of /proc.
.TP
\fB\-\-dump\fR
Write one JSON snapshot to standard output and exit. The snapshot carries
every value the interface draws, so a bug report and a script see the same
facts.
.TP
\fB\-\-pretty\fR
Indent \fB\-\-dump\fR output for human reading. Ignored without \fB\-\-dump\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-devices\fR
List the discovered AMD GPUs as JSON and exit.
.TP
\fB\-\-kind\fR \fIauto\fR|\fIigpu\fR|\fIdgpu\fR
Force every device to be classified as integrated or discrete instead of
using the discovery heuristics.
.TP
\fB\-\-theme\fR \fINAME\fR
Colour theme: default, dracula, gruvbox, monochrome or radeon. The same set
is available as \fIui.theme\fR and can be cycled with the \fBm\fR key.
.TP
\fB\-\-no-color\fR
Never emit colour, even on a colour terminal. The same as \fIui.no_color\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-no-processes\fR
Skip the /proc scan, which is the most expensive part of a sample. The
process table then reports that it was not read, rather than appearing idle.
.TP
\fB\-\-blocks\fR
Force the optional per-block panel on, overriding \fIblocks.enabled\fR. The
panel is fed by a supervised \fIradeontop\fR child.
.TP
\fB\-\-no-blocks\fR
Never start radeontop, even when the configuration enables the panel.
Mutually exclusive with \fB\-\-blocks\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-log\fR \fIPATH\fR
Record the session to PATH while the interface runs. The suffix decides the
format: .csv, .json, .jsonl or .ndjson; a trailing .zst or .zstd compresses
with zstd. Records are appended and flushed one at a time, so an interrupted
session is still readable. Cannot be combined with \fB\-\-check\fR,
\fB\-\-dump\fR or \fB\-\-devices\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-check\fR
Report which metrics this machine can provide and why any are missing: kernel
release against the driver floor, gpu_metrics ABI recognition, per-attribute
presence, process-table ownership, render nodes and the radeontop panel.
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Emit the \fB\-\-check\fR report as JSON. Ignored without \fB\-\-check\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-version\fR
Print the version and exit.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B q
Quit, saving the interface state first.
.TP
.B tab
, \fBshift+tab\fR
Next / previous GPU.
.TP
.B o
Overview: every GPU at once.
.TP
.B 1\-9
Jump to GPU 1 to 9.
.TP
.B R
Sample now.
.TP
.B +
, \fB\-
Faster / slower sampling.
.TP
.B m
Cycle the colour theme.
.TP
.B p
Show or hide the process table.
.TP
.B s
, \fBS\fR, \fBr\fR
Next / previous sort column, reverse the direction.
.TP
.B /
Filter processes; \fBesc\fR clears the filter.
.TP
.B a
Show or hide idle clients.
.TP
.B t
Nest the table by parent process.
.TP
.B c
Show the full command line instead of the short name.
.TP
.B up
, \fBdown\fR
Move the process selection.
.TP
.B k
Signal the selected process. Disabled unless \fIprocess.allow_kill\fR is
true, and always asks for confirmation naming the target.
.TP
.B h
, \fB?\fR
Open or close the help overlay.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/gputop/gputop.yaml
User configuration. Every key is optional; see gputop.example.yaml.
.TP
.I ~/.config/gputop/config.yaml
Session state written by gputop itself (theme, interval, sort, filter, view
choices). The write is atomic and edits the parsed document in place, so
comments and key order survive. \fB$GPUTOP_STATE\fR overrides the path.
.TP
.I /sys/class/drm/card*/device/
VRAM/GTT, busy percentage, DPM clocks, PCIe link, gpu_metrics, SCPP profile
and overdrive tables. Read-only.
.TP
.I /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon*/
Temperatures, power and fan. Read-only.
.TP
.I /proc/<pid>/fdinfo/
Per-process DRM accounting: resident VRAM/GTT and cumulative engine time.
Read-only. Unreadable files for other users are reported as a partial view.
.TP
.I /dev/dri
Optional render nodes, listed by \fB\-\-check\fR when present. The process
table needs a node; sysfs monitoring does not.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GPUTOP_CONFIG
Configuration file to load before the default location.
.TP
.B GPUTOP_STATE
File the session state is read from and written to.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B gputop
Start the terminal interface.
.TP
.B gputop --check
Explain what this machine can show and why anything is missing.
.TP
.B gputop --dump --pretty
Print one complete snapshot for a bug report.
.TP
.B gputop --devices
List the AMD cards discovery found.
.TP
.B gputop --log session.csv.zst
Record the interface to a compressed CSV while it runs.
.TP
.B gputop --theme gruvbox --no-blocks
Pick a palette and leave the optional radeontop child alone.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
Success, including a headless mode that answered its question.
.TP
.B 1
Usage error, unreadable configuration that could not be defaulted, or an
unexpected failure. The message names the cause.
.TP
.B 3
No AMD GPU was found. This is a fact about the machine, not a failure of
gputop, and lets a script distinguish "no hardware" from "broken".
.SH NOTES
The optional blocks panel starts radeontop as a child. It is off by default
because an unprivileged radeontop usually answers "are you root?". The rest
of the monitor needs no privileges.
.PP
An unknown gpu_metrics revision, including any 3.x layout, is never guessed
at: the readers fall back to sysfs and \fB\-\-check\fR names the revision.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR radeontop (1),
.BR btop (1)
